      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The rigorous mathematical foundation of the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is deeply rooted in the non-commutativity of quantum mechanical operators corresponding to conjugate variables, such as position ($\\hat{x}$) and momentum ($\\hat{p}$). Specifically, the uncertainty product is derived from the general uncertainty relation for any two observables, $\\hat{A}$ and $\\hat{B}$: $\\Delta A \\Delta B \\geq \\frac{1}{2} |\\langle [\\hat{A}, \\hat{B}] \\rangle|$, where $[\\hat{A}, \\hat{B}] = \\hat{A}\\hat{B} - \\hat{B}\\hat{A}$ is the commutator. For position and momentum, the canonical commutation relation is $[\\hat{x}, \\hat{p}] = i\\hbar$, which directly yields the minimum uncertainty product $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\hbar/2$. This relationship is intrinsically linked to the properties of the wavefunction ($\\psi$), as the uncertainty in an observable is defined by the standard deviation of the corresponding operator acting on the state vector: $\\Delta A = \\sqrt{\\langle \\hat{A}^2 \\rangle - \\langle \\hat{A} \\rangle^2}$. Furthermore, the principle has",
      "source": "target",
